&lt;h2&gt;What is a CRM for insurance companies?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A CRM for insurance companies is a system that tracks customer contacts, quotes, and policy lifecycles, enabling agents to sell policies and serve customers more effectively. Modern insurance CRM platforms integrate with email and phone systems, and most also connect directly with carrier tools so agents can work in one place instead of jumping between systems.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The insurance industry still relies on fragmented, legacy systems. Some are using filing cabinets for customer contacts, while others have to switch between completely separate systems for sales, policy documents, and customer service. Insurance CRM systems help agents close more deals and reduce manual data entry by organizing leads and automating routine tasks.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;CRM vs. AMS vs. Agent Portal for Insurance&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Insurance teams use three types of software (CRM, AMS, and Agent Portal), each serving a different purpose.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CRM (Customer Relationship Management) &lt;/strong&gt;focuses on sales and relationships. The system manages leads, tracks opportunities, and automates marketing campaigns. Sales teams use CRM to move prospects from initial contact to closed policy.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AMS (Agency Management System)&lt;/strong&gt; handles policy administration and back-office insurance operations. The system processes policies, manages commissions, handles accounting, and stores official documents. Service teams use AMS for policy changes, claims, and renewals.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Agent portal &lt;/strong&gt;supports producer workflows such as quoting and binding. Carriers provide portals where agents compare rates, submit applications, and issue policies.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
